Lower full-warp 64-bit integer sum reductions using three 32-bit `redux.sync` operations. Reduce the low and middle 16-bit chunks and the high 32-bit word separately, then combine the results with 64-bit additions.

Idea:
```cpp
uint32_t low = redux_add(uint32_t(x) & 0xffff);
uint32_t middle = redux_add(uint32_t(x) >> 16);
uint32_t high = redux_add(uint32_t(x >> 32));

result = uint64_t(low) + (uint64_t(middle) << 16)
       + (uint64_t(high) << 32);
```

Each 16-bit subtotal fits in 21 bits across 32 lanes, so the final additions preserve carries and 64-bit wraparound.

On GB300, measured latency improves by **1.93%, 1.53%, and 4.08%** across three configurations, with unchanged register usage and no spills.

